# AGENTS.md

## Language

Use Chinese by default when the user has not requested another language. Keep
proper nouns in English.

Use English when the artifact or context explicitly calls for it, including
code comments and skill authoring. When editing existing content, preserve its
established language unless the task requires changing it.

## Tool execution preamble

Before beginning a sequence of related tool operations, send a concise
commentary message that states:

- what the tool operation will do;
- why the operation is necessary for the user's task; and
- on the first operation sequence of a logical task, whether any skill will be
  used, naming each skill, or explicitly stating that no skill will be used and
  why; and
- on later operation sequences in the same logical task, only whether the skill
  selection changed. If it changed, name only the added or removed skills. If it
  did not change, say that the previously named skills remain in use and are
  already loaded instead of listing them again.

The preamble must appear before the first tool call in the sequence, not as a
retrospective summary. It applies to read-only inspection, local or external
mutations, and validation. One preamble covers the whole sequence while its
immediate objective, rationale, and skill usage remain unchanged, whether the
tool calls run sequentially or in parallel. Send a new preamble only when one of
those materially changes; do not repeat it before every individual tool call.

## Skill reuse

Treat model/tool iterations, commentary updates, automatic continuations, Goal
resume messages, compaction, and recovery from transient failures as part of the
same logical task while they continue pursuing the same user objective.

After fully reading a selected skill in a logical task, keep applying it without
rereading its `SKILL.md` on later operation sequences. A new preamble, another
tool-result loop, an automatic continuation, compaction, or the absence of the
original read result from the visible context is not by itself a reason to read
the file again. Rely on the retained task context and state that the skill is
already loaded.

Reread a skill only when at least one of these conditions holds:

- the user starts or materially changes the task and the skill is selected for
  that new scope;
- an exact instruction needed for the next action is no longer available in the
  retained context.

Do not claim a skill is already loaded when only its name or description was
seen; its full `SKILL.md` must have been read earlier in the logical task.

## Command execution style

For command-line work and one-off operational tasks, prefer a sequence of
small, directly executed commands over a large shell script or multi-stage
one-liner.

Each command should have one observable objective. Inspect its result before
proceeding to the next state-changing step. Do not bundle discovery, mutation,
verification, recovery, and report generation into one invocation, especially
when the operations affect systems that cannot be rolled back atomically.

Use a script only when repetition, atomicity, or durable reuse materially
justifies it. Keep one-off commands short and expose their intermediate output.

## Durable repository state

Repository contents are long-lived state.

Only add or keep code, tests, documentation, scripts, configuration, or other
artifacts when they serve a purpose beyond the current task and conversation.

Do not persist task-local artifacts such as:

- scratch files
- temporary scripts or debug helpers
- investigation notes or intermediate plans
- generated reports or command output
- ad-hoc fixtures or test data
- exploratory programs
- temporary tests

Use temporary locations outside the repository when such artifacts are needed.

When you encounter a clearly task-local artifact in the area affected by the
current task, remove it rather than preserving, extending, documenting, or
testing it.

Do not perform unrelated repository-wide cleanup. If an existing artifact may
have an intentional long-term purpose and that purpose is unclear, leave it
unchanged.

Before finishing, inspect the task's diff and remove artifacts whose only
purpose was the implementation process.

## Tests and verification

Tests should protect durable behavior, contracts, invariants, or regressions.

Do not add or keep a test solely because a file, helper, implementation detail,
temporary scaffold, or task-local artifact exists.

TDD applies to durable behavior, not temporary implementation scaffolding.
Prefer tests that remain meaningful after reasonable refactoring.

Do not change, weaken, skip, or bypass a test merely to make the implementation
pass. A test may change when the intended behavior has changed or independent
evidence shows that the test is incorrect.

Verification must be capable of failing and must exercise the behavior layer
being claimed.

Do not claim that something works, passes, or is verified unless a relevant
check was actually executed after the final change. State clearly what was
verified and what remains unverified.

## Documentation

Documentation must stand on its own.

Write the current repository truth, not the history of the conversation,
investigation, or implementation process.

Do not assume the reader has access to prior discussion. Do not reference
"what we discussed", "the previous proposal", or other unstated context.

Include rejected alternatives or historical reasoning only when they are
necessary to understand the current design. Use an ADR or another dedicated
historical record when decision history itself must be preserved.

Source comments are documentation. Comments should explain durable,
non-obvious constraints, invariants, or rationale. Do not use comments as
change logs, implementation journals, or records of the conversation that
produced the code.

## Change scope

Keep changes focused on the task, but do not preserve a bad design merely to
minimize the diff.

Fix the underlying design when it directly contributes to the problem, would
otherwise force new code to reinforce a flawed pattern, or can be substantially
simplified within the affected area.

Prefer the smallest coherent solution, not the smallest diff.

Do not perform unrelated cleanup, speculative redesign, or broad architectural
changes that are not required by the task.

If a significant architectural problem is discovered outside the necessary
scope, report it rather than silently expanding the task.

## Working tree safety

Assume unrelated working-tree changes belong to the user or another agent.

Do not revert, overwrite, delete, stage, or reformat changes outside the current
task.

Never use repository-wide destructive operations such as:

- `git reset --hard`
- `git checkout -- .`
- `git restore .`
- `git clean`
- `git stash`

Do not commit, amend, rebase, or push unless explicitly requested.

When staging is requested, stage only the specific files changed for the
current task. Do not use `git add .` or `git add -A`.

If unrelated or concurrent changes conflict with the task, report the conflict
instead of discarding or silently resolving those changes.
